<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mạc Thiên Tứ</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1780">1780</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mac Thien Tu, Mo Tianci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mạc Thiên Tứ was the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ha-tien">Hà Tiên</a> principality on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-cuu">Mạc Cửu</a> in 1735 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a>. A patron of letters, he gathered Chinese and Vietnamese poets in a literary society and intervened in Cambodian successions. Driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of Siam in 1771, he later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, where he died in 1780.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mac-thien-tu.jpg" alt="Mạc Thiên Tứ" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%E1%BB%99_M%E1%BA%A1c_Thi%C3%AAn_T%E1%BB%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
